Explain shoulder function and evaluate a clinical claim (@shoulder-review: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @shoulder, #source @med-source-applied, #source @med-source-cuff-guideline, #source @med-source-fimpact, #medicine @shoulder-advanced-anatomy, #orthopedics @shoulder-advanced-anatomy, #shoulder @shoulder-advanced-anatomy, #medicine @shoulder-bones, #orthopedics @shoulder-bones, #shoulder @shoulder-bones) { r_0KVS4CSNH1J97G8XKXEW5183JW
Bring together the anatomy, function, problem categories, and evidence without using your own pain as a diagnostic exercise.

1. Reconstruct the region in anterior, posterior, and section views: bones, the three true joints, scapulothoracic movement, labrum, capsule, ligaments, tendon courses, bursae, muscles, nerves, and main vessels.
2. Explain an overhead reach and a rotation through anatomy, muscle coordination, and loading. Name the simplifications in your explanation.
3. Compare fictional cuff pain, stiffness, instability, arthritis, and a nerve-related presentation. State what is compatible, what remains unknown, and what would require assessment.
4. Interpret an annotated imaging example, distinguishing finding, diagnosis, and treatment implication.
5. Read one guideline recommendation and one trial, identifying population, outcome, uncertainty, and applicability. Explain a tempting overgeneralization and correct it.
6. Produce a short English/Portuguese glossary and a source-supported account a lay reader could follow.

This review completes the introductory-to-intermediate shoulder reading, not professional clinical training. It should require real study and revisiting sources. The following readings deepen anatomical detail and research skills rather than attaching a credential or treatment authority.

**Return to the initial rotational question:** write a clear fictional account of the movement and compare a cuff explanation, a stiffness or instability explanation, and a labral interpretation. Use [Work through a rotational-pain question with focused readings](A-rotational-pain-question.lingua#r_5GSTPQ2M46MJAY4PTWARZ8N31S) to select exact passages. Explain why pain at an end position does not by itself identify structural damage, and what evidence would be needed to strengthen or reject each account.

